Optional `segment_coordinate_divisor` for meshes published in full-resolution voxels.

Why: slabs are sampled at `spacing` segment units, and every length constant in the
dataset (ray length, merge distance, match tolerance) is in those units, so segment
units must equal the voxel size the model was trained at (about 9.6 um, i.e. level 2
of a 2.4 um scan). The released config reads PHerc0139 with segment_downscale 4,
which only works with meshes already divided by 4. The published
`<id>-on-<volume>-2.399um.tifxyz` meshes are in full-resolution voxels; read as-is
(segment_downscale 1) the model sees 4x zoomed slabs and scores precision 0.13 and
winding count error 2.9 instead of 0.44 and 0.41.

What: a per-dataset `segment_coordinate_divisor` (default 1, unchanged behaviour)
divides valid vertex coordinates on load. Use it together with segment_downscale,
e.g. {"volume_scale": 2, "segment_downscale": 4, "segment_coordinate_divisor": 4}.

Checked: published meshes with divisor 4 give labels identical to pre-divided copies
on 8 PHerc0139 slabs (division by 4 is exact in float32).

